S2="This section installs what is missing and makes the system changes that the experiment needs. My repository does not cover these changes, so they are the most important part of this guide."
STEPS={
'm01':("Install the packages","I installed the compilers, the autotools used by OpenVPN and OpenSSH, the headers of OpenSSL, zlib and libcap-ng, and python3-venv for the analysis. The first sudo asks for the cybsec password.","apt ends without an error. The next steps print the version of each tool."),
'm02':("Install Go 1.24.7","wireguard-go needs Go, and the Ubuntu package is too old for it. So I installed the official archive. dpkg --print-architecture fills in arm64 by itself.","The last line must read go version go1.24.7 linux/arm64."),
'm03':("Load the BBR module","Ubuntu ships BBR (Cardwell et al., 2016) as a module that is not loaded. Without it, the BBR campaign cannot work. This does not survive a reboot.","lsmod lists tcp_bbr."),
'a01':("Confirm the machine and the user","Every prompt in this guide reads cybsec@cyborg, so I checked the names first.","The first two lines read cyborg and cybsec, and sudo appears in the group list."),
'a02':("Record the operating system","Results are only comparable when the platform is known.","Ubuntu 24.04.3, kernel 6.8.0-149-generic, aarch64. A later kernel of the 6.8 series is fine."),
'a03':("Record the CPU and memory","The scripts pin the client to CPU 0 and the server to CPU 1, so two CPUs are required. The last line counts the CPUs that expose the AES instructions, because OpenVPN and SSH AES-GCM depend on them.","2 CPUs, about 7.7 GiB of memory and a last line of 2. The model name is empty inside a QEMU guest."),
'a04':("Check the build tools","A missing tool shows up here as command not found, which is easier to fix now than in the middle of a build.","Each line prints a version."),
'a05':("Check the runtime tools","This covers OpenSSL, Python, Go, ip and tc, the TUN device used by all tunnels and by the emulator, and the helper programs that the scripts call.","Nothing is missing, and go version prints go1.24.7."),
'a06':("See which congestion controls exist","The experiment compares CUBIC (Ha et al., 2008) and BBR, so both must exist.","bbr and cubic appear on the first line, and the default is cubic."),
'a10':("Allow BBR inside the namespaces","This fix is not in my repository, and it matters. A network namespace can only select a congestion control listed in tcp_allowed_congestion_control, and Ubuntu lists only reno and cubic. Without this step, exp_loss.py ignores the error, BBR is never set, and the BBR results would really be CUBIC. This setting does not survive a reboot.","The allowed list contains reno, cubic and bbr."),
'a07':("Look at the root account","The SSH tunnels log in as root with a key. The lab sshd has no PAM support, and without PAM it refuses an account whose password field starts with !, which means locked.","The line starts with root:. On my VM it was already root:*, and on another install it may be root:!. The next step handles both."),
'a08':("Make root able to log in with a key","The value * means that no password can ever match. So only key login becomes possible, and password security does not get weaker.","The output reads root:*."),
'a09':("Create the sshd user","sshd refuses to start without a user named sshd for privilege separation. Because I did not install the Ubuntu OpenSSH server, this user did not exist, so the first line shows an error before the command creates it.","The last line shows a uid for sshd."),
'b01':("Create a folder for the sources","I built the tools from source at fixed versions, so that the same code is tested each time.","The command prints /home/cybsec/src."),
'b02':("Download OpenVPN 2.7.8","This clones only the tagged release and moves into the new folder.","The commit is dc1f569."),
'b03':("Configure OpenVPN","autoreconf generates the configure script, and configure checks the system. The output goes to log files in ~/src. --disable-dco keeps OpenVPN in user space, which is the point of the comparison (Quartulli, 2022).","Both lines end in exit=0. If not, I would read ~/src/ovpn-configure.log."),
'b04':("Build and install OpenVPN","I installed the binary directly. sudo make install also installs the man pages, which need rst2man, and it stopped with exit code 2 on my first testbed.","make exit=0, the path /usr/local/sbin/openvpn, and OpenVPN 2.7.8 with aarch64 in the version line."),
'b05':("Download OpenSSH 10.6p1","Same idea as {b02}.","The commit is a8c72b8."),
'b06':("Configure OpenSSH","The prefix /usr/local keeps this build apart from the Ubuntu ssh client. No PAM option is given, which is why {a08} was needed.","Both lines end in exit=0."),
'b07':("Build and install OpenSSH","install-nokeys skips the system host keys. The lab has its own keys.","Both exit codes are 0. which prints the /usr/local paths, and ssh -V prints OpenSSH_10.6p1."),
'b08':("Download iperf 3.22","iperf3 measures the throughput.","The commit is 926bd90."),
'b09':("Build and install iperf3","ldconfig matters. iperf3 uses a shared library in /usr/local/lib and fails without it.","All three exit codes are 0, and the last line reads iperf 3.22."),
'b10':("Download wireguard-tools","This gives the wg command, which generates WireGuard keys and configures interfaces.","The commit is 49ce333."),
'b11':("Build and install wireguard-tools","The three WITH_...=no options skip parts I do not use.","Both exit codes are 0, and the version is v1.0.20260223."),
'b12':("Download wireguard-go","This is the user-space WireGuard of the study. I checked out a fixed commit.","The commit is b3bc704."),
'b13':("Build and install wireguard-go","Go downloads its libraries on the first build. I used sudo install instead of sudo make install, because sudo does not see /usr/local/go/bin.","make exit=0 and version v0.0.20250522-3-gb3bc704."),
'c01':("Download the benchmark repository","Commit 2184184 is the one I tested.","The first line shows 2184184, followed by the file list."),
'c02':("Copy the lab scripts to /opt/lab","The scripts have the path /opt/lab written into them.","13 files are listed."),
'c03':("Compile the link emulator","impair is the C forwarder that drops packets and adds delay between two TUN devices in rtr.","The command prints /usr/local/bin/impair."),
'c04':("Create the folders for keys and results","The keys folder is private to root.","Both folders exist, and keys shows drwx------."),
'c05':("Generate the WireGuard keys","The repository holds no keys. I generated one key pair for the server and one for the client.","Four files are listed."),
'c06':("Generate the SSH keys","I created one host key and one client key. The file names are the ones that sshd_config and tun.sh expect.","Nine files are listed."),
'c07':("Create the certificate authority","OpenVPN uses certificates, so I created a small private CA (RSA-4096, SHA-256).","Subject and issuer are both CN = lab-ca."),
'c08':("Create the server certificate","The keyUsage line is required. tun.sh starts the client with --remote-cert-tls server, which checks that extension. Without it, the handshake fails with VERIFY KU ERROR.","The output shows Key Usage and TLS Web Server Authentication."),
'c09':("Create the client certificate","This is the same procedure, for client authentication.","The output shows TLS Web Client Authentication."),
'c10':("Create the tls-crypt key and verify","tc.key encrypts the OpenVPN control channel, which makes OpenVPN silent to unauthenticated probes. chmod protects the private files, and openssl verify checks both certificates.","Both certificates print OK, and 21 files are listed."),
'd01':("Build the three namespaces","This creates cli, rtr and srv with their veth pairs, turns on forwarding and sets CUBIC.","exit=0 and three namespace names."),
'd02':("Check the addresses","Each namespace should hold its addresses.","10.0.1.2 in cli, 10.0.1.1 and 10.0.2.1 in rtr, 10.0.2.2 in srv. vc1 shows DOWN, and it is still working, as the next steps prove."),
'd03':("Check the congestion control","All three namespaces should start on CUBIC.","Three lines of cubic."),
'd035':("Prove that BBR can be selected","This is a one-time test of {a10}. The scripts ignore errors from this setting, so a direct test is the only way to be sure.","The output shows bbr, then cubic. Operation not permitted means {m03} and {a10} must be repeated."),
'd04':("Start the test servers","This starts the iperf3 server on port 5201 and the latency echo server on port 7000 inside srv.","Ports 5201 and 7000 are listening."),
'd05':("Measure the raw link","This 3-second test has no tunnel and no impairment. It proves that the namespaces and routing work.","Tens of Gbit/s on this kind of host. I reached 107,842 Mbit/s."),
'd06':("Measure the raw latency","This runs 3,000 rounds of 64 bytes.","One JSON line. median_us is the median round trip, 27.08 µs on my run."),
'd07':("Turn on the impaired link","This inserts 1% loss, 10 ms delay each way and the 100 Mbit/s bottleneck, so I could see the emulator working before relying on it.","An impair process, a tbf queue at 100 Mbit and the two policy rules for tables 100 and 200."),
'd08':("Measure through the impaired link","With 1% loss, plain CUBIC should collapse far below 100 Mbit/s (Mathis et al., 1997).","A few Mbit/s. I measured 5.12 Mbit/s at the receiver."),
'd09':("Remove the impairment","The next tests need the clean link again.","exit=0, impair stopped, and noqueue instead of tbf."),
'd095':("Restart the test servers","The next test starts from a clean state.","Ports 5201 and 7000 are listening."),
'd10':("Smoke-test all seven tunnels","This brings up each tunnel, runs 3 seconds of iperf3 and tears it down. A problem with keys, certificates or accounts shows up here, not an hour into a campaign. timeout 15 is there because iperf3 waits forever on a dead tunnel.","All seven lines show a rate, and finished is printed. A blank rate means that the tunnel did not come up (see Section X)."),
'e00':("Start from a clean state","This removes any leftover tunnel and shows that the results folder is empty and that the namespaces use CUBIC. New runs are added to the end of old files, so old files must go.","No result files, and three lines of cubic."),
'e01':("Run the clean-link campaign","This runs eight configurations in random order for 5 trials. Each trial measures setup time, 20 s of one-stream throughput, 20 s of four streams (first 3 trials), and latency at 64 and 1024 bytes. The progress goes to ~/clean.log.","exit=0, DONE and a log of 185 lines."),
'e02':("Run the loss sweep with CUBIC","This runs six configurations, 3 trials and six loss rates (0, 0.1, 0.5, 1, 2 and 5%) on a 100 Mbit/s link with 10 ms of delay each way.","exit=0, DONE and a log of 127 lines."),
'e03':("Check for failed runs","At 5% loss, a TCP connection can stall so badly that iperf3 returns no summary. The script then records an error and 0 Mbit/s. That 0 is a failed measurement and not a result, so it must be repeated.","The first number is 108 (6 configurations × 6 loss rates × 3 trials). The second number is the failures. I had 2, both SSH-L at 5%. Another run may differ."),
'e035':("Create the re-run script","The repository has rerun.py, but its list is written into the code and only matches my older failures. This small script finds the failures in the results file and repeats each one with the same tunnel, loss and seed, up to 3 times. analyze.py keeps the latest record of each run. Python needs the indentation, so the file is also in testbed.zip.","The file has 24 lines."),
'e036':("Check the script","The first command checks the Python syntax, and the checksum shows that the file is identical to mine.","exit=0 and the same checksum."),
'e04':("Re-run the failed CUBIC runs","The script does nothing when there is no failure.","The list of failed runs, one line per new measurement, DONE and exit=0."),
'e05':("Run the loss sweep with BBR","This runs five configurations, 3 trials, at 1% and 5% loss. It is the campaign that needs {a10}.","exit=0, DONE and a log of 46 lines. Raw TCP at 1% should be near 90 Mbit/s, not a few Mbit/s."),
'e055':("Re-run any failed BBR runs","This is the same clean-up as for CUBIC.","failed runs: [] on my run, then DONE and exit=0."),
'e06':("Run the security probes","This plays an attacker in rtr without keys. It sends junk to each listener and records whether anything comes back.","WireGuard and OpenVPN-UDP with tls-crypt show reply false. OpenVPN without tls-crypt replies with 26 bytes, the behavior used by Xue et al. (2022). SSH shows its banner, SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.6."),
'e07':("Run the context-switch measurement","This runs three trials for each of six configurations and counts context switches per MB delivered.","exit=0, DONE and a log of 19 lines."),
'm04':("Prepare Python for the analysis","Ubuntu ships a matplotlib older than what requirements.txt asks for, so I used a private environment with fixed versions. The lines below must stay in the same shell, because later steps use the python3 of this environment.","pip ends with Successfully installed, and the last line reads 2.5.3 3.11.2."),
'f01':("Make a working copy","The repository holds the published results. I worked on a copy so that I could compare the two.","The command prints /home/cybsec/rerun."),
'f02':("Replace the published results","The new results belong to root, so I changed the owner.","Ten files owned by cybsec."),
'f03':("Run the analysis","analyze.py computes the means and 95% confidence intervals and writes analysis/summary.json and analysis/ctx.json.","One line per configuration, then the loss tables. Table 5 explains the fields."),
'f04':("Regenerate the figures","These scripts draw the report figures into figures/.","All four exit codes are 0, and seven .png files are listed. The two numbers of fig_ctx.py are r and the slope of Figure 4 in the report."),
'f045':("Create the comparison script","This small script is mine and is not in the repository. It is also in testbed.zip.","19 lines and the same checksum."),
'f05':("Compare with the published results","This prints the new numbers next to the x86 numbers stored in the repository.","Table 4 below explains how to read this output."),
'x02':("Save the results","I copied everything to the shared folder before tearing down. tar leaves the keys out on purpose. The folder must be mounted (Section 3.4).","The listing shows analysis, figures, results and the logs."),
'f06':("Tear the testbed down","This removes the tunnels, the servers and the three namespaces. Results stay in ~/rerun.","No namespace is listed before done."),
'x04':("Measure the vCPU clock","Metric M6 in my report needs the clock f, and a QEMU guest does not report it. So I timed chains of dependent additions (1 cycle each) and multiplications (3 cycles each) on each vCPU.","About 4.0 GHz on both vCPUs on my Mac. A different Mac gives a different value, and report metric M6 then uses that value."),
'x08':("Count the source lines","Table 5 of my report compares code size. I counted core code only with cloc: Go files of wireguard-go without tests and other operating systems, C files of src/openvpn, and C files of OpenSSH without regress, openbsd-compat and contrib.","Three numbers: 8705, 87135 and 122763."),
'x05':("Confirm the negotiated key exchange","Table 5 of my report lists the SSH key exchange, so I confirmed it on this build.","kex: algorithm: mlkem768x25519-sha256."),
}
SECTIONS=[
("IV- PREPARING THE SYSTEM",'4',S2,['m01','m02','m03','a01','a02','a03','a04','a05','a06','a10','a07','a08','a09']),
("V- BUILDING THE TOOLS",'5',"I built five programs at the versions of my report. Each build writes to a log file in ~/src, so the screen shows only the exit codes. The value exit=0 means success.",['b01','b02','b03','b04','b05','b06','b07','b08','b09','b10','b11','b12','b13']),
("VI- INSTALLING THE LAB AND CREATING THE KEYS",'6',"The repository contains the scripts but no keys, and it does not explain how to create them. So the commands below are my own, and the file names are the ones the scripts expect. I recommend generating fresh keys and never reusing keys from another machine.",['c01','c02','c03','c04','c05','c06','c07','c08','c09','c10']),
("VII- CHECKING THE TESTBED",'7',"Before spending 1 hour 40 minutes on measurements, I checked each piece on its own.",['d01','d02','d03','d035','d04','d05','d06','d07','d08','d09','d095','d10']),
("VIII- RUNNING THE CAMPAIGNS",'8',"I ran these steps one at a time, in this order. The campaign commands print nothing until they finish, which is normal. On a real terminal, tmux keeps them alive if the connection drops: tmux new -s lab starts a session, and tmux attach -t lab returns to it. I did not need tmux because my recorder ran directly on tty1.",['e00','e01','e02','e03','e035','e036','e04','e05','e055','e06','e07']),
("IX- ANALYZING THE RESULTS",'9',"This section turns the raw records into tables and figures, and then compares them with the published data.",['m04','f01','f02','f03','f04','f045','f05']),
]
